<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Jawor</h1><p class="st-meta">1274 CE – 1392 CE · capital Jawor</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lower Silesian Piast duchy at the foot of the Sudetes, later joined with Świdnica, which kept its independence from Bohemia longer than any other.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1274 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-legnica">Duchy of Legnica</a> was divided among the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-the-bald">Bolesław II the Bald</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-i-the-strict">Bolko I the Strict</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawor">Jawor</a> with its lands. He soon added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swidnica">Świdnica</a> and built a strong duchy on the Bohemian border, protected by castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolkow-castle">Bolków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodziec-castle">Grodziec</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bolko I founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krzeszow-abbey">Krzeszów Abbey</a> and fortified the mountain passes. After his death the lands were divided, with Henry I ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawor">Jawor</a> until 1346. They were then reunited under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-ii-the-small">Bolko II the Small</a>, who resisted Bohemian overlordship, ruling as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> prince while other Silesian dukes became Bohemian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. German settlers and town law shaped the economy of farming and cloth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-ii-the-small">Bolko II</a> married his niece Anne to Charles IV, so on the death of his widow Agnes in 1392 the duchy passed to the Bohemian crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawor">Jawor</a> later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and has been Polish since 1945; its Lutheran Peace Church is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-jawor.jpg" alt="Duchy of Jawor" width="320" height="298"><figcaption>Image: ŚląskiMaper · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_%C5%9Al%C4%85ska_z_ksi%C4%99stwami.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1274 CE – 1392 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jawor</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bolko I the Strict, Henry I of Jawor, Bolko II the Small</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Jauer</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1333&amp;focus=duchy-of-jawor" aria-label="Duchy of Jawor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5048 407 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Jawor lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5368 663L5367 659M5368 663L5330 692M5368 663L5375 670"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5367 659h0M5330 692h0M5375 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5368 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5368 663h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jawor. <a href="/atlas?year=1333&amp;focus=duchy-of-jawor">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
